33,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"I have found that catalogue-style projects tend to have a lasting impact, while those focused on specific mechanisms often remain unresolved. Also, projects relying on breeding colonies are more prone to schedule overruns.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"My findings indicate a shortage of phenotyping technicians, though there is high interest in modelling posts. However, the senior modelling analysts are currently seconded abroad, while the glasshouse crew remains at full strength.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The shared spectroscopy suite suffered significant downtime last year due to contract lapses, and the glasshouse blocks are currently booked solid for the next two growing cycles, though the animal facility is well-supported.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the financial landscape is shifting. While the ministry is kindly subsidizing glasshouse space, animal-unit rates have spiked. Interestingly, simulation and modelling hours are now provided at no charge under the new agreement.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've learned that field sites have been quite unstable recently due to flooding and fire, though indoor facilities are fine. On a brighter note, the national repository is now processing deposits much faster than before.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"It seems we have a complex puzzle. @Ryuji, @Victoria, and @Benjamin, your findings regarding the glasshouse bookings and staffing suggest that projects relying on those facilities might face significant hurdles.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Victoria"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shizue, that is correct. While the glasshouse crew is available, the lack of phenotyping technicians and the full bookings Victoria mentioned create a bottleneck that could jeopardize any project relying on those specific resources.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shizue"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. Furthermore, the instability of the spectroscopy suite suggests that any proposal heavily dependent on high-end shared instrumentation may face unpredictable delays, regardless of the available staffing.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a fascinating irony that while the ministry subsidizes the glasshouse space, the space itself is unavailable. We must be careful not to let a subsidy blind us to a physical impossibility.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, that's a very good point. Between the instrumentation downtime and the field site instability I found, it seems that projects relying on external or shared physical environments are currently the most vulnerable to interruptions.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"It is heartening to see us sharing these insights so openly. It seems we should be wary of projects that depend on the glasshouses or unstable field sites, as well as those relying on breeding colonies.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"We should also be cautious about projects requiring specialized phenotyping technicians or senior modelling analysts, as the current staffing gaps could lead to significant delays or failure in execution.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. We must prioritize a proposal that aligns with current capacities—specifically avoiding the overbooked glasshouses and the unreliable spectroscopy suite—to ensure the research can actually be completed.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed. If we prioritize stability, we should favor projects that leverage the free computing hours and the efficient repository, while avoiding the costly vivarium and the unavailable glasshouses. Logic suggests a computational or data-driven approach is safest.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I agree with Benjamin. Given the risks to field sites and the bottlenecks in the glasshouses, a project that relies more on data and computation seems the most reliable choice for this cycle.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with you all. It seems we are in accord that a project with a lasting, catalogue-style impact and minimal reliance on fragile physical infrastructure would be the most prudent choice.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I concur. We must prioritize a proposal that is realistically executable given the current staffing shortages and facility constraints, favoring stability and long-term utility over high-risk experimental setups.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. We have a clear set of criteria now: we should avoid the glasshouses, the spectroscopy suite, and unstable field sites, while favoring projects that are computationally viable and sustainable.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have reached a most elegant consensus. We shall seek a candidate that avoids the logistical nightmares of the glasshouses and vivariums, favoring instead the efficiency of the digital realm.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree completely. It feels like we've found a way to protect the research from the kinds of interruptions that have hindered so many projects in the past. I'm ready to see the candidates.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
