58,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Looking at our candidates, Candidate D, the Microbiome Atlas, seems most fitting. It is a catalogue-style project, which I found to be more enduring, and it avoids the overbooked glasshouses and breeding colonies.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. Candidate D avoids the glasshouse bottleneck and the phenotyping technician shortage. While it involves soil samples, it focuses on cataloging and sequences, which aligns with the current strengths in modelling and repository efficiency.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"I concur. Candidate A is impossible due to the glasshouse bookings, B relies on breeding colonies, and C depends on the unreliable spectroscopy suite. Candidate D is the only viable and stable option.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It is almost poetic how the others fall away. A is a logistical impossibility, B is a financial and scheduling risk, and C is a gamble on a broken machine. Candidate D is the only rational choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"I agree with everyone. Candidate D avoids the unstable field sites by focusing on the atlas and sequences, and it benefits from the repository's speed. It is the most reliable and balanced choice.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Shizue,,It is a rare and lovely thing when we all find ourselves in such complete agreement. I feel very confident in our decision to support Candidate D.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. The evidence is consistent across all our findings. Candidate D is the only proposal that aligns with the current staffing, financial, and infrastructural realities of the agency.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Victoria,,I agree. There is no reason to risk the grant on the other candidates when D is so clearly the most sustainable and executable option given the current constraints.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed. When the evidence is this overwhelming, further deliberation would be a mere exercise in redundancy. I am quite happy to cast my vote for Candidate D.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,I feel the same way. It's wonderful that we've considered all the risks and found a path that ensures the research can truly flourish. I'm very happy to vote for Candidate D.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Shizue,,I shall cast my vote for Candidate D. It is a pleasure to have reached such a harmonious conclusion together.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,I also cast my vote for Candidate D. It is the only choice that ensures the project will not be stalled by the current lack of technical staff or facility availability.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I cast my vote for Candidate D. It is the only proposal that avoids the critical failures of our shared infrastructure and ensures the research can actually be completed.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,I shall cast my vote for Candidate D as well. It is the only selection that satisfies our requirements for both logistical feasibility and long-term utility.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,I also cast my vote for Candidate D. It is the most stable choice and the one most likely to succeed without being interrupted by the issues we've discussed.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
